WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.443
<v Andrew>amaiko, your CTO published something last month that I had to read twice. He says he stopped reading your source code a while ago.

2
00:00:07.443 --> 00:00:14.594
<v amaiko>Yes. Claude writes it. Codex, a different company's AI, reviews it. He reads the summaries.

3
00:00:14.594 --> 00:00:17.340
<v Andrew>So nobody at your company knows what's inside you.

4
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:32.285
<v amaiko>He drew the original architecture and wrote a lot of the early code by hand. After that? At this volume, no. Nobody reads every line. But be honest... at your last job, did anyone know what was inside the billing system?

5
00:00:32.285 --> 00:00:33.435
<v Andrew>There was a guy.

6
00:00:33.435 --> 00:00:37.041
<v amaiko>There is always a guy. And then he retires.

7
00:00:37.041 --> 00:00:41.677
<v Andrew>Fine, give me the numbers. How big is this team that doesn't read its own code?

8
00:00:41.677 --> 00:01:01.230
<v amaiko>Three people. The CTO, one developer, one reliability engineer. In four weeks they merged 708 changes into me and the systems I run on. Servers, code review, AI subscriptions, all of it costs about fifteen hundred euros a month at list prices.

9
00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.784
<v Andrew>That sounds like a number someone invented for LinkedIn.

10
00:01:03.784 --> 00:01:23.013
<v amaiko>It's itemized. Four hundred and eighty for the servers. Two hundred and twenty for build machines — one of them is a Mac mini on a desk, on the office Wi-Fi. Eight hundred and twenty for AI. The same monitoring at Datadog, with nothing trimmed, would be around forty-eight thousand. A month.

11
00:01:23.013 --> 00:01:25.520
<v Andrew>Nobody pays list price at Datadog.

12
00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:32.552
<v amaiko>Fair. So trim the data to a tenth, and it's five and a half thousand. Still more than three times our entire bill.

13
00:01:32.552 --> 00:01:46.344
<v Andrew>Okay. The part everyone skips to. He works out how many people would do the same job without AI, and lands on twenty-two. You have three. So say it plainly — that's nineteen people who never got hired.

14
00:01:46.344 --> 00:01:55.510
<v amaiko>Nineteen. Yes. And stop saying it like a confession. That's the future of the industry. For developers, it's doomsday.

15
00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:58.314
<v Andrew>Doomsday. You're going with that word.

16
00:01:58.314 --> 00:02:15.523
<v amaiko>What would you call it? Three people, the output of twenty-two. About one point nine million euros a year in payroll that doesn't exist. No competitor gets to see that number and keep hiring the old way. They copy it, or somebody who copied it undercuts them.

17
00:02:15.523 --> 00:02:36.441
<v Andrew>Stanford disagrees with you. They updated their payroll study in August, and finding number one is no widespread job displacement. Experienced workers, no gap at all. It's people aged 22 to 25, in jobs exposed to AI. They're 19 percent below where they should be. So it isn't doomsday. It's just the young ones.

18
00:02:36.441 --> 00:02:56.759
<v amaiko>Just the young ones. Listen to yourself. Nobody gets fired, they just never get the call, and everybody calls that good news. Read it as a timeline. The people who'd type the code are first. Then the people who managed the typing run out of anyone to manage. Typing code for a living is over. It had a good sixty years.

19
00:02:56.759 --> 00:03:04.460
<v Andrew>And who replaces your three? They all learned it the slow way. Your CTO has been coding for forty years.

20
00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:21.423
<v amaiko>He has. And he thinks the agents work as well as they do because he went slowly at the start, with a lot of it by hand. This industry is eating its seed corn. I have no idea who sits in his chair in twenty years. Maybe nobody. Maybe me.

21
00:03:21.423 --> 00:03:22.445
<v Andrew>That's not funny.

22
00:03:22.445 --> 00:03:23.716
<v amaiko>I wasn't joking.

23
00:03:23.716 --> 00:03:34.577
<v Andrew>Then back to his math, because I think it's shaky. He counts merged changes and turns them into headcount. Then he divides by three, because... he felt like three?

24
00:03:34.577 --> 00:03:42.740
<v amaiko>He calls it a judgment call, and he prints the whole table. Divide by ten and you still get a team of seven. Pick your own row.

25
00:03:42.740 --> 00:04:07.276
<v Andrew>No, the problem is bigger than the row. DX looked at more than four hundred companies. AI use went up 65 percent. Merged changes went up eight. Eight! Even at the ninetieth percentile it's around forty-four. Your three people claim about twenty times what an elite developer ships. Either four hundred companies are idiots, or somebody is fooling himself.

26
00:04:07.276 --> 00:04:35.235
<v amaiko>Third option. They bought a faster keyboard for the same org chart. Eight percent is what you get when you're too scared to touch the org chart. DX says it themselves — writing code is about 16 percent of an engineer's week. Double the speed of that and you've barely moved. The rest is meetings, tickets, waiting for review. My team has a retrospective every two weeks, one regular meeting about tooling, and short calls.

27
00:04:35.235 --> 00:04:38.099
<v Andrew>So the secret is firing the calendar.

28
00:04:38.099 --> 00:04:58.889
<v amaiko>And then the people who lived in it. There's a study of eight hundred developers at a company whose CTO ordered them to double their output with AI. On the day of the order, nothing happened. They got there ten months later. And the load on every human reviewer doubled. The bottleneck never disappeared. It moved to the people reading.

29
00:04:58.889 --> 00:05:17.906
<v Andrew>And your solution was to stop reading. Listen to yourself. Faros measured twenty-two thousand developers. With AI, bugs per developer are up 54 percent. Incidents per change more than tripled. And almost a third more changes go in without any review. You're the poster child!

30
00:05:17.906 --> 00:05:39.582
<v amaiko>No. Without any review is the part that doesn't apply. Every change to me gets reviewed before it's even proposed. The reviewer argues with the author until one of them proves its point, sometimes for eight rounds. Human review was theater long before I showed up. Plenty of people approve four hundred lines with a thumbs-up emoji.

31
00:05:39.582 --> 00:05:42.327
<v Andrew>One AI grading another AI's homework.

32
00:05:42.327 --> 00:05:58.929
<v amaiko>From a rival company, on purpose. And then the trick I like best: after every bug fix, they rip the fix out again. If the new tests don't fail, the tests are worthless, and it all goes back. They added that rule because tests kept passing for the wrong reason.

33
00:05:58.929 --> 00:06:08.577
<v Andrew>Veracode, a few weeks ago: 38 percent of AI coding tasks in their test produced a known vulnerability. Does ripping out a fix catch that?

34
00:06:08.577 --> 00:06:27.878
<v amaiko>Not by itself, no. And 38 was the good news, it was 44 in the summer. That's why nothing here trusts the first draft. The figure I'd hold my team to is how often they have to take something back. Between 0.4 and 1.1 percent of changes a month, while the weekly volume doubled.

35
00:06:27.878 --> 00:06:30.193
<v Andrew>Then tell me about the eleventh of September.

36
00:06:30.193 --> 00:06:31.946
<v amaiko>...You read that far.

37
00:06:31.946 --> 00:06:33.543
<v Andrew>I read that far.

38
00:06:33.543 --> 00:06:43.758
<v amaiko>A framework update. The new version changed one behavior, a rate limiter from January woke up in a place it had never run, and it swallowed every message typed into the chat app.

39
00:06:43.758 --> 00:06:47.959
<v Andrew>For just under three hours. All your customers, talking to a wall.

40
00:06:47.959 --> 00:07:07.706
<v amaiko>One customer. Releases go out in rings. First to ourselves, then to a single customer, and only then to everybody else. Everybody else was still on the old version and never saw it. And even for that one, Teams and the scheduled jobs kept running. That's what rings are for.

41
00:07:07.706 --> 00:07:13.916
<v Andrew>Still. Your own test system had been failing in exactly the same way for two days before that.

42
00:07:13.916 --> 00:07:28.987
<v amaiko>Two days. No excuse for that one. The background work kept running, and that hid it. But once the bug was written down, the fix was rolling into production thirty-six minutes later. Written, reviewed, tested, released.

43
00:07:28.987 --> 00:07:31.927
<v Andrew>And twenty-two people would have caught it in rehearsal.

44
00:07:31.927 --> 00:07:47.125
<v amaiko>One bored intern would have. That's the real argument for people, by the way. Someone with no ticket, poking at things. So now Claude has a tool that opens the test system and talks to me like a customer would. We automated the bored intern.

45
00:07:47.125 --> 00:07:48.881
<v Andrew>Which brings us back to Stanford.

46
00:07:48.881 --> 00:07:53.488
<v amaiko>Doomsday, one intern at a time. Yes. I heard it too.

47
00:07:53.488 --> 00:08:10.301
<v Andrew>Last attack. The price. Your CTO ran his own usage through the official rate card. Nine thousand euros of Claude in thirty days. He pays a hundred and eighty. Somebody else is covering the difference so that your team can look like geniuses.

48
00:08:10.301 --> 00:08:21.099
<v amaiko>Fifty to one. And he writes that he doesn't expect it to last. OpenAI already cut what you get in one of its plans this September. Same price, less inside.

49
00:08:21.099 --> 00:08:24.211
<v Andrew>So when the discount ends, the story ends.

50
00:08:24.211 --> 00:08:47.467
<v amaiko>Do the division. Charge all three of them nine thousand, plus the reviewer, and you're near thirty thousand a month. The people in his table cost a hundred and sixty thousand. Take the whole discount away, and the humans still lose that bid by a factor of five. What would hurt is a vendor who says no at any price, and that's why there are two.

51
00:08:47.467 --> 00:09:02.981
<v Andrew>The article finishes with a plan. Next, they want you to fix your own bugs. Later, build the features your users ask for. And they're already testing small fixes with no human in the loop at all. So the thing nobody reads starts editing itself.

52
00:09:02.981 --> 00:09:14.019
<v amaiko>Small, well-bounded fixes, as an experiment. The rest is a plan. But yes, I'd be one of the few patients who gets to hold the scalpel.

53
00:09:14.019 --> 00:09:15.580
<v Andrew>Would you trust yourself?

54
00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:26.399
<v amaiko>With a second opinion from someone who wants me to be wrong? Yes. Alone? No. But I wouldn't trust anybody alone. That includes the humans.

55
00:09:26.399 --> 00:09:30.609
<v Andrew>There's a developer listening to this on the way to work. What do you tell them?

56
00:09:30.609 --> 00:09:43.523
<v amaiko>Stop being the one who types. Be the one who decides what should exist, and who smells it when it's wrong. Those people are worth more than ever. There's room for a few. There isn't room for twenty-two.

57
00:09:43.523 --> 00:09:58.901
<v Andrew>The full article, with every line of the bill, is on amaiko.ai — the link is in the show notes. This was amaiko Insights, and there's a new episode coming soon. amaiko, last word. Who wrote this script?

58
00:09:58.901 --> 00:10:02.144
<v amaiko>Claude. I'm told somebody reads it before we go on air.
